---
import Docs from '../../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import Callout from 'web-design-system/astro/marketing/Callout.astro'
import CodeBlock from 'web-design-system/astro/product/CodeBlock.astro'
import TableScroll from '../../../components/atoms/TableScroll.astro'
import { Badge } from 'web-design-system/components'

const toc = [
  { label: 'Current status', href: '#status' },
  { label: 'Run from source', href: '#source' },
  { label: 'Build a bundle', href: '#bundle' },
  { label: 'Platforms', href: '#platforms' },
  { label: 'The release gate', href: '#gate' },
  { label: 'Signing and updates', href: '#signing' },
]

const platforms = [
  { platform: 'macOS', status: 'Development bundle; release gate pending', detail: 'macOS 13+; app bundle. Signing and notarization are not complete.' },
  { platform: 'Linux', status: 'Development package; release gate pending', detail: 'Ubuntu 24.04+; Debian package. WebKitGTK 2.44+ is required.' },
  { platform: 'Windows', status: 'Planned', detail: 'No supported build or installer yet.' },
]

const bundle = `# macOS app bundle
direnv exec . pnpm --dir apps/shepherd build --bundles app

# Ubuntu Debian package
direnv exec . pnpm --dir apps/shepherd build --bundles deb`
---

<Docs
  title="Releases"
  description="What Shepherd ships today, how to build it yourself, and what the clean-machine release gate still requires."
  toc={toc}
>
  <Callout tone="warning" title="There is no signed release yet">
    Shepherd runs from a checkout. The macOS and Linux release gate has not been run, and signed
    installers are a next-step item. A development bundle is not a release.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="status">Current status</h2>
  <p>
    <Badge tone="warning" variant="subtle">pre-release</Badge> The repository builds a debug macOS app
    bundle and a debug Debian package for packaging checks. The manually dispatched package smoke
    verifies those bundles on clean runners without launching the app; it is preflight, not the
    release gate.
  </p>

  <h2 id="source">Run from source</h2>
  <p>
    The supported way to use Shepherd today is from a checkout:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock filename="terminal" code={'direnv allow .\npnpm install\nshep'} showLineNumbers={false} />
  <p>
    The <a href="/docs/users/install">install page</a> covers test mode, agent setup and the first
    project. If you are working on the code, the contributor guide has the test and release commands.
  </p>

  <h2 id="bundle">Build a bundle</h2>
  <p>
    The app build pins the macOS deployment target to 13.0. Use a clean target directory when checking
    a release so cached objects cannot change the result.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock filename="terminal" code={bundle} showLineNumbers={false} />
  <p>
    <code>cargo test -p shepherd --test packaging</code> checks that the Tauri config, build script,
    CI workflows and platform settings still agree. It does not replace a clean-machine run.
  </p>

  <h2 id="platforms">Platforms</h2>
  <TableScroll label="Shepherd release platforms">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>Status</th><th>Details</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {platforms.map((entry) => (
          <tr><td>{entry.platform}</td><td>{entry.status}</td><td>{entry.detail}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TableScroll>

  <h2 id="gate">The release gate</h2>
  <p>
    A release needs clean machines, not a developer checkout. The gate runs the Rust and frontend
    suites, builds the macOS app and Ubuntu package from scratch, checks the minimum system version,
    confirms no <code>/nix/store</code> path leaks into the bundle, installs the package, adds a
    project, starts a task, answers an approval and inspects the diff.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="/docs/contributors/release-pipeline">release pipeline page</a> lists the workflows
    and artifact checks. The checklist's evidence table is still pending for both platforms, so a
    release is not ready even when the preflight passes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="signing">Signing and updates</h2>
  <p>
    Signing and notarization are human-gated. Until they are configured, macOS Gatekeeper will warn
    about an unsigned build. v1 has no updater: you replace the app from the source or a new bundle
    yourself. Windows support, a signed Windows installer and a downloads page are planned with no
    date.
  </p>
</Docs>
